1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,500
Production is broken. Dinner shouldn't be.

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:12,200
You've spent eight hours debugging, your brain is running on fumes, and your fridge has four ingredients that don't look like a meal.

3
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:17,800
The last thing you need is a recipe that demands another hour of work.

4
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:24,800
Meet SlothChef: an extreme low-effort cooking companion built for people who want dinner, not a second project.

5
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:30,500
Give it what you've got. Let it help you figure out what you can actually make.

6
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:37,800
Guided by the Sloth Contract: strictly one cooking vessel, zero cutting boards, and under five minutes of active standing.

7
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:44,500
Let's try the post-deploy pantry: instant ramen, peanut butter, soy sauce, and a little sriracha.

8
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,200
SlothChef turns those ingredients into a practical starting point, explains what goes into the dish, and identifies what you might still need.

9
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:59,800
Notice how the recipe uses the ingredients we entered: no imaginary pantry, no unnecessary shopping list, and no elaborate restaurant workflow.

10
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:07,500
It synthesizes the Midnight Dan-Dan Rescue: one skillet, one fork, and three minutes of active cooking.

11
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:14,800
With radical shortcuts: crush garlic flat with a coffee mug, and tear greens with your bare hands.

12
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:22,800
And Cook-Along mode reads each step aloud with an audible timer, so you never touch your phone with dirty hands.

13
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:28,500
Behind the interface is the engineering that makes the experience possible.

14
00:01:28,900 --> 00:01:34,800
A request moves from the React frontend into the FastAPI backend, through a decoupled dual-AI pipeline.

15
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:44,200
First, Prior Labs' TabPFN tabular foundation model evaluates continuous chemical flavor vectors—umami, salt, acidity, and lipids—in a single Bayesian forward pass under 45 milliseconds.

16
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:48,800
It mathematically quantifies synergy before generating a single word.

17
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:54,800
Then, Google's open-weight Gemma 2 model translates those exact physical bounds into sequential, zero-friction cooking instructions.

18
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:57,800
Mathematical precision meets culinary empathy.

19
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:04,500
SlothChef is deployed live on Render via a multi-stage Docker container with automated health monitoring.

20
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:08,200
Why does open innovation matter here?

21
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:11,500
Because midnight pantry habits are intensely personal.

22
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:16,500
Using open-weight models means private fridge telemetry never leaves your device.

23
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:22,800
It runs 100% offline with zero token anxiety, proving that open AI can solve real, unglamorous human problems without cloud lock-in.

24
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:27,500
The result? A steaming bowl of rich, spicy Dan-Dan noodles.

25
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:35,200
Thirty-eight minutes reclaimed, six fewer dirty dishes to wash, and an edible victory when energy was at absolute zero.

26
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:41,800
Because conservation of energy isn't laziness—it's the supreme evolutionary virtue.

27
00:02:42,200 --> 00:02:45,200
Production is broken. Dinner shouldn't be.

28
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:49,800
Try SlothChef live on Render or fork the code on GitHub. Built for DEV Hacktoberfest 2026: Slow and Steady.
